   "file": "TauCeti/Topology/Algebra/Group/Profinite/ZHat/Decomposition.lean",
   "fix": "State the general lemma and derive the idempotent case, e.g. `theorem IsIdempotentElem.ne_intCast {a : Additive zHat.{u}} (ha : IsIdempotentElem a) (h0 : a \u2260 0) (h1 : a \u2260 1) (n : \u2124) : a \u2260 n` (or the iff `IsIdempotentElem a \u2192 ((\u2203 n : \u2124, a = n) \u2194 a = 0 \u2228 a = 1)`), then `theorem idem_ne_intCast (n : \u2124) : idem.{u} \u2113 \u2260 n := (isIdempotentElem_idem \u2113).ne_intCast (idem_ne_zero \u2113) (idem_ne_one \u2113) n`. The integer-reduction step (`Int.cast_injective` + `IsIdempotentElem.iff_eq_zero_or_one`) is unchanged and uses only `CharZero (Additive zHat)` from TauCeti/Topology/Algebra/Group/Profinite/ZHat/ZMod.lean:220.",
   "issue": "The proof uses nothing about `idem \u2113` beyond `isIdempotentElem_idem`, `idem_ne_zero`, `idem_ne_one` (lines 303-309), so it proves only a special case of: an idempotent of `Additive zHat` that is an integer is `0` or `1`. The roadmap's own 0.1 checklist names a second instance of this general fact (\"the element with components `(1, 0, 1, 0, \u2026)` in `\u220f \u2124_\u2113`, which is not an integer\"), which is likewise idempotent and \u2260 0, 1 \u2014 it is not covered by the current statement.",
   "line": "301"
  }
